  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe region remains under the influence of a broad upper-level \ntrough in place across the Pacific Northwest. Embedded waves of \nenergy will continue to rotate through this upper-level pattern,\nbringing waves of cloud cover and precipitation to parts of \neastern Idaho over the coming days. Early morning satellite \nimagery shows the next wave of clouds and moisture positioned \nacross eastern Oregon, which will progress toward Idaho \nthroughout the morning. High-resolution convective-allowing \nmodels (CAMs) show this feature producing multiple rounds of \nprecipitation across the Central Mountains \u2014 especially around \nthe Sawtooths \u2014 over the course of the next 48 hours, albeit \nwith occasional breaks. Fire Zone 422 remains under a Red Flag \nWarning today due to anticipated thunderstorm coverage.\n\nOutside of the Central Mountains, the rest of eastern Idaho \nlooks dry through Friday, making wind the primary operational \nhazard. Winds will push Wind Advisory and Lake Wind thresholds \ntoday. The current wind forecast has been nudged upward closer \nto the NBM 90th percentile guidance, placing expected gusts \nright on the cusp of advisory criteria. Given the short duration\nand marginal nature of the setup, we have opted to hold off on \na formal advisory for now, though a short-fused issuance may be \nneeded this afternoon if winds overperform. Temperatures today \nwill be noticeably cooler across the region, with daytime highs \nlimited to the 60s and 70s.\n\nWinds will remain breezy on Thursday and Friday, but latest \nguidance suggests speeds will be a touch lower than what we \nexperience today. A slight warming trend will take hold during \nthis period, with maximum temperatures increasing by 2 to 4 \ndegrees each day from Wednesday through the weekend.\n\nAs we move into the latter half of the Labor Day holiday\nweekend, global models show the aforementioned Pacific upper low\nslowly starting to make eastward progress toward Idaho. This\nwill allow for increased atmospheric lift and deep moisture to\npush into the area on Sunday, and especially on Monday.\nPrecipitation chances are trending upward and look increasingly\nlikely for the holiday. While there is still a bit of\nuncertainty regarding the exact track of the low, confidence is\nincreasing for a potentially unsettled Labor Day across the\nforecast area. Extensive cloud cover, widespread precipitation,\nand lower 500 mb heights should result in well-below-normal high\ntemperatures on Labor Day and through the first part of next\nweek, dropping 10 to 15 degrees below seasonal norms.",
